34
00:02:58.729 --> 00:03:05.025
Methods should be described well enough for
readers to evaluate the work.

35
00:03:05.025 --> 00:03:12.249
Consider this worked case: A lab manual
includes exact software versions, hardware

36
00:03:12.249 --> 00:03:16.463
specs, and step-by-step instructions for
running experiments.

37
00:03:16.463 --> 00:03:23.561
Compare alternatives against the same
constraints so the evaluation does not move

38
00:03:23.561 --> 00:03:24.744
the goalposts.

39
00:03:24.744 --> 00:03:31.697
Using shared criteria turns a preference into
a defensible technical choice.

40
00:03:31.697 --> 00:03:38.584
Name each dependency explicitly, and keep
labels and representations consistent

41
00:03:38.584 --> 00:03:40.650
throughout the analysis.

42
00:03:40.754 --> 00:03:48.188
Apply Accurate Results Reporting to a worked
situation instead of treating it as an

43
00:03:48.188 --> 00:03:49.250
isolated definition.

44
00:03:49.250 --> 00:03:55.870
Results should be reported accurately rather
than selected to create a misleading

45
00:03:55.870 --> 00:03:56.422
conclusion.

46
00:03:56.422 --> 00:04:03.946
Consider this worked case: A graph includes
error bars and a confidence interval to show

47
00:04:03.946 --> 00:04:06.454
the variability of experimental outcomes.

48
00:04:06.454 --> 00:04:13.616
Work from the concrete inputs to the result,
checking units, types, or invariants at each

49
00:04:13.616 --> 00:04:14.094
step.

50
00:04:14.094 --> 00:04:19.235
The worked case shows both how to apply the
method and how to check it.

51
00:04:19.346 --> 00:04:26.425
Examine the tradeoffs in Limitations and
Threats to Validity, including what improves

52
00:04:26.425 --> 00:04:29.374
and what becomes more difficult.

53
00:04:29.374 --> 00:04:34.655
Limitations and threats to validity should be
made visible.

54
00:04:34.655 --> 00:04:42.633
Consider this worked case: A thesis section
explicitly states sample size constraints and

55
00:04:42.633 --> 00:04:45.702
potential biases in user testing.

56
00:04:45.702 --> 00:04:52.389
State the benefit, the cost, and the condition
under which the tradeoff is acceptable.

57
00:04:52.389 --> 00:04:59.592
Making the tradeoff visible prevents a local
improvement from hiding a larger cost.

58
00:04:59.592 --> 00:05:06.671
Name each dependency explicitly, and keep
labels and representations consistent

59
00:05:06.671 --> 00:05:08.795
throughout the analysis.

60
00:05:08.906 --> 00:05:16.358
Stress-test the reasoning around Formatting
Graphs, Figures, and Tables with an edge case

61
00:05:16.358 --> 00:05:20.371
and identify which assumption controls the
outcome.

62
00:05:20.371 --> 00:05:23.371
Computing professionals should avoid harm.

63
00:05:23.371 --> 00:05:30.194
Consider this worked case: A table uses
consistent column alignment, clear headers,

64
00:05:30.194 --> 00:05:33.606
and units for every numerical value.

65
00:05:33.606 --> 00:05:40.543
Change one assumption at a time and observe
which part of the reasoning no longer holds.

66
00:05:40.543 --> 00:05:47.621
The edge case reveals the boundary of the
claim without discarding the useful core idea.

67
00:05:47.738 --> 00:05:55.351
Use Teamwork and Peer Review to diagnose a
failure by separating symptoms, causes, and

68
00:05:55.351 --> 00:05:56.438
corrective action.

69
00:05:56.438 --> 00:06:03.531
Professionals should strive to achieve high
quality in both processes and products.

70
00:06:03.531 --> 00:06:11.255
Consider this worked case: A group uses a
structured peer review form to check for

71
00:06:11.255 --> 00:06:14.344
logical gaps, citation accuracy, and clarity.

72
00:06:14.344 --> 00:06:22.347
Locate the first point where observed behavior
diverges from expected behavior, then test the

73
00:06:22.347 --> 00:06:24.062
smallest plausible cause.

74
00:06:24.062 --> 00:06:31.578
The diagnosis is strongest when another person
can reproduce the observation and the test.

75
00:06:31.682 --> 00:06:38.431
Connect Ethical Decision-Making Framework to
the earlier ideas and explain how the pieces

76
00:06:38.431 --> 00:06:40.507
work as one system.

77
00:06:40.507 --> 00:06:48.815
Ethical computing decisions identify
stakeholders, foreseeable benefits and harms,

78
00:06:48.815 --> 00:06:52.507
uncertainty, alternatives, and obligations.

79
00:06:52.507 --> 00:07:01.433
Consider this worked case: A team evaluates a
new AI tool by mapping affected users,

80
00:07:01.433 --> 00:07:06.194
potential biases, data privacy risks, and
alternative approaches.

81
00:07:06.194 --> 00:07:15.756
Explain each connection explicitly and verify
that information retains the same meaning

82
00:07:15.756 --> 00:07:17.350
across representations.

83
00:07:17.350 --> 00:07:24.913
The synthesis matters only if every connection
remains supported by the original evidence.

84
00:07:25.034 --> 00:07:31.885
Finish with Practical Analysis Checklist as a
practical checklist that can guide a new

85
00:07:31.885 --> 00:07:32.374
analysis.

86
00:07:32.374 --> 00:07:38.963
They should maintain high standards of
professional competence, conduct, and ethical

87
00:07:38.963 --> 00:07:39.562
practice.

88
00:07:39.562 --> 00:07:47.664
Consider this worked case: A final assignment
requires students to audit a sample research

89
00:07:47.664 --> 00:07:54.608
paper using the checklist to verify claims,
methods, formatting, and ethical compliance.

90
00:07:54.609 --> 00:08:02.503
For each checklist item, record the evidence,
the remaining uncertainty, and the next

91
00:08:02.503 --> 00:08:03.718
verification step.

92
00:08:03.718 --> 00:08:10.295
The result is a repeatable procedure, not a
conclusion that depends on memory or

93
00:08:10.295 --> 00:08:10.765
